Simple version:  Is there a way to get the recover utility as compiled
in the same context as the Mac OS X Terminal Port nethack binary found
on http://www.nethack.org/v343/ports/download-mac.html?

Longer more detailed question:

I installed the "Mac OS X Terminal Port" nethack binary from
http://www.nethack.org/v343/ports/download-mac.html.

I got a GREAT game going to the castle (when the shopkeeper on level
three asks if you would like to pay 700g for that wand you just picked
up, you say *YES*).

My battery died, and my computer failed to hibernate properly.

I realized I had no recover utility (not installed by the installer
app in any obvious location, at any rate).

I downloaded the source and compiled the recover utility.

It "couldn't find the level of the save".  I added printf statements
and recompiled, and discovered it was looking not for level 26, but
for level (26 * 2^24).  Somehow the endianness of my compiled recover
binary/system and the endianness of the base.0 record file don't
agree.  =(

I can solve this in one of three ways:

1) I can figure out how to invert the endianness of the recover
utility.  I'm not sure how to do that, but if anybody can offer
guidance that would be great.

2) I can invert the endianness of the record file.  That sounds very
difficult, though, because I don't know which bytes are part of
integers.

3) I can get a copy of the recover utility that matches my nethack
installation, and presumably that would already match endianness with
its sibling.

I don't know what causes the endian disagreement, but I'm suspicious
that the Terminal port mentioned above is compiled for PPC instead of
Intel (which disagree on endianness).

Any help greatly appreciated!  This was all set up to be my third
ascension, and my first Tourist, so I'm motivated to get this fixed...

